+## Receipt taxonomy — every removal needs exactly one
 
-## Decision tables
+Four receipts can be `SAFE`. No receipt → no removal. Two receipts →
+either suffices; the tools try them in different orders and may label
+different receipts for the same path. Note both. A line copied from an
+earlier census is not a receipt.
+
+### `pushed`
+
+Qualifies when the path exists and its **introducing** commit is an
+ancestor of the resolved remote-tracking default ref. Not the last
+commit. Not a hardcoded branch name.
+
+```
+git -C <repo> log --diff-filter=A --format=%H -1 -- <path>
+```
+
+Default ref, both tools:
+
+1. `git symbolic-ref --quiet --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD`, strip a
+   leading `origin/`, and require `refs/remotes/origin/<name>` to exist.
+2. Else, if exactly one `refs/remotes/origin/*` ref exists besides
+   `HEAD`, use that ref.
+3. Else pushed is unverifiable. `refs/heads/*`, including
+   `refs/heads/master`, never establishes pushed.
+
+Label split, same `NOT-SAFE` verdict: `souji-verify.sh` sets receipt
+`pushed-unverifiable`. `souji-vault-verify.sh` sets receipt `none` and
+`NOT-VERIFIED(none)`, with evidence containing `pushed=unverifiable`.
+A missing `pushed-unverifiable` label on the vault tool is not a pass.
+
+A repo-root (a directory containing `.git`) is never `SAFE(pushed)`.
+It is `SAFE` only via `moved-to-obsidian`; otherwise
+`NOT-SAFE(unpushed)`, `NOT-SAFE(dirty)`, or `NOT-SAFE(none)`, with
+`unpushed=N dirty=M` in the evidence.
+
+`souji-verify.sh` does not consult the ledger for a missing path
+(`NOT-SAFE(unreachable)`). Ancestry of a path that no longer exists is
+not a receipt.
+
+Do not hardcode `origin/main`. Resolve. A one-liner that fetches and
+tests `origin/main` is not this receipt.
+
+**Operator gate.** `SAFE(pushed)` does not prove the worktree blob still
+matches the introduced blob, and does not prove later commits touching
+the path are pushed. Require `git status --porcelain -- <path>` empty,
+then re-run the verifier.
+
+### `moved-to-obsidian`
+
+Receipt of record: `tools/souji-vault-verify.sh --fallback`, not an
+`awk` of the ledger. A row qualifies only when all of these hold:
+
+- source path and vault path are absolute (relative → `row-incomplete`)
+- both sha256 fields are lowercase hex, 64 characters (uppercase →
+  `row-incomplete`)
+- source bytes re-hash to column 2 and vault bytes re-hash to column 4
+- source path ≠ vault path (`source-equals-vault`)
+- the vault path is not inside the source directory
+  (`vault-inside-source`)
+- a directory digest contains no symlink (`symlink-present` in either
+  tool)
+
+`souji-verify.sh` still attempts a ledger match, but it does not enforce
+absolute paths, lowercase hex, self-row refusal, or vault-inside-source,
+and it skips missing paths. `SAFE(moved-to-obsidian)` from that tool
+alone is not this receipt.
+
+**Known limit, not a tool behavior to rely on.** A ledger vault path
+with a secret-shaped component can still be hashed. Do not author such
+a row. If one exists, the verdict is not authorization to read it —
+route the path to kagi and do not remove.
+
+### `stale`
+
+Scratch family, either tool, any one of:
+
+- a path component named exactly `.scratch` (any file under it)
+- basename `*.out` or `*.log`
+- basename `*-draft.*`
+- basename `*checkpoint*.json`, `*checkpoint*.png`, `*ckpt*.json`,
+  `*ckpt*.png`, `*.ckpt`, or `launch-*.json`
+
+Not scratch by themselves: `*.tmp` outside `.scratch`, a directory named
+`out/`, a `reports/` tree whose basename does not match the patterns
+above. A config snapshot is not scratch. If the verifier says `pushed`,
+the receipt is `pushed`. "Not a byte-identical duplicate" is a note, not
+a different receipt.
+
+Age: mtime age in seconds greater than `--stale-days` × 86400. Default
+`--stale-days` is 1. This is not "older than the owning lane's last
+admitted round".
+
+Live references, from the owning repo (or `SHOKUNIN_ROOT` if the path
+is not inside a repo). Zero lines required. The artifact itself is
+excluded:
+
+```
+rg -l --no-ignore --fixed-strings -e <basename> . \
+  -g '!.git' -g '!**/.git/**' \
+  -g '!souji-shokunin/census/**' -g '!**/souji-shokunin/census/**' \
+  -g '!souji-shokunin/tests/**' -g '!**/souji-shokunin/tests/**' \
+  -g '!**/.ufo-quarantine/**'
+```
+
+**Operator gate (known false-SAFE).** That sweep omits `--hidden`, so a
+reference that lives only in a hidden file is invisible and the tool can
+still emit `SAFE(stale)`. Before any removal, re-run the same sweep with
+`--hidden`. Any hit → `NOT-SAFE`, whatever the tool printed.
+
+**Known limit.** The sweep uses `--no-ignore` and does not skip
+secret-shaped paths. Do not run a stale check whose search root contains
+secret-shaped files. Refusing the target path does not protect files the
+sweep opens.
+
+### `metadata-prune`
+
+Worktree registration only. The path equals a non-main `worktree <path>`
+line from `git -C <owner> worktree list --porcelain`. Owners scanned:
+`$SHOKUNIN_ROOT` and `$SHOKUNIN_ROOT/*-shokunin`.
+
+Order is load-bearing. The named-branch check runs first:
+
+1. Named branch and `git -C <owner> log --oneline <branch> --not --remotes`
+   count > 0 → `NOT-SAFE(unpushed-branch)`, even when the directory is
+   absent and unique commits against `--all` are 0.
+2. Directory absent and `git -C <owner> log --oneline <HEAD> --not --all`
+   count = 0 → `SAFE(metadata-prune)`.
+3. Directory absent and unique > 0 → `NOT-SAFE(none)`.
+4. Directory present and dirty → `NOT-SAFE(dirty)`.
+5. Directory present and clean → `NOT-SAFE(none)`. A live worktree is
+   not prunable metadata.
+
+This receipt authorizes removing that registration's metadata only. It
+does not authorize deleting the branch, the commits, or sibling content.
+Executing a prune is parent/operator. Seats do not write sibling repos.
 
